# Didache Chapter 8 — The EMET Bible (Literal)

**Translator:** EMET | **Source:** Codex Hierosolymitanus (H), Didache 8 | **Source Image:** source-texts/greek-EC/DID_008_page_001.jpg

---

## Chapter 8

**1** But do not let your fasts be with the hypocrites — for they fast on the second and fifth days of the week; but fast on the fourth day and on the Preparation.

**2** And do not pray as the hypocrites, but as the Lord commanded in his gospel, pray thus:

> Our Father, who art in heaven,
> hallowed be thy name;
> thy kingdom come;
> thy will be done,
> as in heaven, so also on earth;
> give us today our daily bread;
> and forgive us our debt,
> as we also forgive our debtors;
> and do not lead us into temptation,
> but deliver us from evil;
> for thine is the power and the glory forever.

**3** Pray thus three times a day.

---

*EMET notes: "Second and fifth days" = Monday and Thursday, traditional Jewish fast days. "Fourth day and Preparation" = Wednesday and Friday (the day of betrayal and the day of crucifixion). "Hypocrites" may refer to Jewish opponents or to a rival Christian faction. "Daily bread" — ἐπιούσιον is notoriously difficult: "daily," "for the coming day," "for existence/sustenance," or "supernatural." "Debt" (ὀφείλημα) matches Matthew 6:12 (not Luke's "sins"). The doxology ("for thine is the power and the glory forever") is not in Matthew's best manuscripts but is present in the Didache — it likely reflects early liturgical expansion. "Three times a day" echoes Ps 55:17 and Dan 6:10.*
